`
			},
			checks: [
				{ id: 'upkeep-exists', kind: 'exists', selector: '#upkeep' },
				{ id: 'no-blanks', kind: 'text', selector: '#upkeep', matches: '^[^…]*$' },
				{ id: 'owner-named', kind: 'text', selector: '#tasks td.who', matches: '[Oo]wner|\\bI\\b|\\bme\\b|[Cc]ontractor|[Ee]ditor|[Dd]eveloper|[Hh]ost|[Aa]gency|[Aa]dmin|[Ss]taff|[Ff]reelanc|[Tt]eam|[Mm]anager' },
				{ id: 'frequency', kind: 'text', selector: '#tasks td.freq', matches: '[Oo]nce|[Ww]eek|[Mm]onth|[Qq]uarter|[Dd]aily|[Yy]ear|[Ee]very|[Aa]nnual' },
				{ id: 'hours', kind: 'text', selector: '#tasks td.hours', matches: '^\\s*\\d+(?:[.,]\\d+)?\\s*$' },
				{ id: 'hours-total', kind: 'text', selector: '#hours-total', matches: '^\\s*\\d+(?:[.,]\\d+)?' },
				{ id: 'backup-where', kind: 'text', selector: '#backup', matches: '(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:[Cc]op|[Bb]ackup))(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:stor|kept|keep|server|cloud|disk|separate|off-?site|elsewhere))' },
				{ id: 'risks', kind: 'count', selector: '#risks li.risk', min: 2 },
				{ id: 'risks-plugin', kind: 'text', selector: '#risks', matches: '[Pp]lugin' },
				{ id: 'compare-rows', kind: 'count', selector: '#compare tr.cms', min: 3 },
				{
					id: 'verdict',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#verdict',
					matches: '(?=[\\s\\S]*\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+)(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:[Kk]eep|[Ss]tay|[Ss]witch|[Mm]ove|[Dd]rop|[Gg]ive up|[Ss]kip|[Rr]eplace|without a CMS|no CMS|Drupal|Joomla|WordPress))'
				}
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'What does the cost of owning a WordPress site add up from?',
				options: [
					'Hosting and a domain name',
					'Paid themes and plugins',
					'Time (or a specialist’s fee) for updates, backups and checks',
					'A fee for every visitor'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 2],
				why: [
					'Yes: they are paid for every year.',
					'Yes: many good plugins and themes are paid, and often by subscription.',
					'Yes: this is the most underrated part. A site nobody looks after sooner or later breaks or gets hacked.',
					'No: visitors cost nothing in themselves. Only the hosting may need to grow.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What are WordPress sites most often broken into through?',
				options: ['Out-of-date plugins and themes with known vulnerabilities', 'The PHP language itself', 'The editor’s browser'],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. The WordPress core is updated carefully, while plugins are written by thousands of different authors, so holes in them appear more often and not everyone closes them in time.',
					'No: PHP itself is not the main cause.',
					'No: the editor’s browser has nothing to do with it.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Why should a backup be tested by restoring it at least once?',
				options: [
					'A copy can come out empty or damaged, and you find out only when you need it',
					'So that the host doesn’t switch the site off',
					'The law requires copies to be tested every month'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. A copy that has never been restored is a hope, not a copy. The test takes an hour and shows what is really saved.',
					'No: the host has nothing to do with it.',
					'No: there is no such general requirement. It is a matter of common sense.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'A site has automatic plugin updates switched on. What trap do they leave?',
				options: [
					'An update can break the site and you won’t learn of it at once, so you need backups and a weekly check of the form',
					'Automatic updates switch off the site’s protection',
					'Automatic updates can only be switched on with a paid plan'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Automatic updates close holes quickly, but a rare conflict happens without you. That is why a backup and a weekly check of the form stay mandatory.',
					'No: on the contrary, they protect, closing known holes without delay.',
					'No: it is a setting of WordPress itself, not of a plan.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each system to where it is most often found today',
				pairs: [
					['WordPress', 'small and medium sites, blogs, shops, service sites'],
					['Drupal', 'big portals with complicated content and permissions (universities, organizations)'],
					['Joomla', 'small sites built on it long ago; its share is shrinking'],
					['No CMS', 'when the content changes twice a year and files are enough']
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'A project’s content changes twice a year, and one developer edits it. What is sensible?',
				options: [
					'Ask whether a CMS is needed at all: a static site made of files is enough, and it has nothing to hack',
					'Install WordPress with a complicated theme anyway',
					'Install as many plugins as possible'
				],
